## Example Usage

Read the onboarding values first, create the role from them, then connect the account:

{{ tffile .ExampleFile }}

`data.act_aws_onboarding_parameters` is a read and creates nothing. Its `deployment_id`, `role_name` and `external_id` are stable, so a plan you run again shows no change.

## Permission groups

Act's role has a core set of permissions plus three optional permission groups, all on by default. When the role leaves a group out, list its capability in `disabled_capabilities` so Act does not expect those permissions. Changing the list updates the deployment in place.

| Capability | Permission group | Without it, Act cannot read |
|---|---|---|
| `eks_cluster_access` | `kubernetes` | Kubernetes objects inside EKS clusters |
| `cloudtrail_logs` | `cloudtrail_logs` | CloudTrail log files in S3 and log events in CloudWatch Logs |
| `ai_services` | `ai_services` | Bedrock, Bedrock AgentCore, OpenSearch Serverless and SageMaker model package group details |

The Act module for AWS, `act-security/act/aws`, takes the groups as `permission_groups` and outputs the list to pass:

```terraform
module "act" {
  source = "act-security/act/aws"

  role_name         = data.act_aws_onboarding_parameters.this.role_name
  external_id       = data.act_aws_onboarding_parameters.this.external_id
  act_principal_arn = data.act_aws_onboarding_parameters.this.act_principal_arn
  permission_groups = { kubernetes = {}, cloudtrail_logs = {} }
}

resource "act_aws_account_deployment" "this" {
  deployment_id           = data.act_aws_onboarding_parameters.this.deployment_id
  account_id              = module.act.account_id
  role_arn                = module.act.role_arn
  external_id_key_version = data.act_aws_onboarding_parameters.this.external_id_key_version
  disabled_capabilities   = module.act.disabled_capabilities
}
```

## Verification

Act assumes the role, checks the account and checks that the trust policy requires the external ID before it stores anything. A new role can take a minute or two to work everywhere in AWS, so while AWS answers `AccessDenied` the provider asks again, backing off up to 30 seconds between attempts, for up to 2 minutes. A failure that cannot fix itself, such as a trust policy without the external ID, stops once the same answer has come back for 30 seconds, which gives AWS time to propagate a trust policy fixed in the same apply. A failed create stores nothing, so the next apply starts clean.

Use `timeouts` to cap the whole operation; the defaults are 5 minutes for create and update.

On success the apply prints a warning with the deployment's console link. It is not an error: it confirms that Act reached the account.

## When the role breaks

If someone deletes the role or changes its trust policy, the next refresh reports `status = "failed"` with a warning, and the plan updates the deployment in place. Once the configuration has fixed the role, the apply asks Act to verify it again and the deployment is active in the same apply. Set `reverify_on_failure = false` to keep the warning without the extra update.

## External ID

Act derives the external ID from the deployment, so it never changes on its own. A deployment created before derived IDs keeps its stored value with `external_id_source = "legacy"`. Set `external_id_source = "derived"` to move it: Act first checks that the role trusts the derived value, `target_external_id` of the onboarding data source, then switches. Update the trust policy before you apply.

## Destroy

Destroying the resource disconnects the account: Act stops scanning and deletes the credentials it held. The role stays in AWS, and the apply lists it in a warning. With `deletion_protection = true`, a plan that destroys or replaces the deployment fails before anything changes; changing `deployment_method` replaces it, so turn protection off and apply that first. To stop managing the deployment without disconnecting it, use a `removed` block with `lifecycle { destroy = false }` on Terraform 1.7 and later, or `terraform state rm`.

